{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-74850",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1))$ on the given curve. We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}+t)⟩ & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}+t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other equation. Therefore, $P(\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1))$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨t,\\,1⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}})⟨t,\\,1⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨t,\\,1⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}[-⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩+({t}^{2}+1)\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0⟩] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,\\,-t⟩ \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{({1}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}‖⟨1,\\,-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
